    ('h2', 'Data classification and the egress contract'),
    ('p', 'Four classes cover every byte in the system. Class A (audio) - original and normalized recordings: never leaves the machine, never enters an export, stored content-addressed in MEDIA_DIR. Class B (raw conversation text) - transcripts and segments: leaves the machine only through the redaction pipeline and only when a task\'s policy allows cloud. Class C (derived intelligence) - insights, scorecards, summaries, drafts: same egress rules as B (they quote the conversation). Class D (operational metadata) - deals, contacts, settings, logs, spend: never sent to providers at all; cloud prompts receive the minimum deal context the task needs (title, stage) and nothing else. The egress contract is the summary invariant: A never egresses; B and C egress only redacted and only per policy; D stays home.'),
    ('h2', 'Key management'),
    ('p', 'Keys are environment variables in .env, loaded once at boot into a frozen config object; nothing downstream re-reads the environment. The file is chmod 600, git-ignored by the scaffold (with a committed .env.example documenting every variable), and never logged - the logger scrubs any string matching known key shapes as a belt-and-braces measure. Rotation is documented per provider in the runbook (replace value, restart, verify with the provider test button); no key rotation requires a database change because no key is ever persisted (NFR-13). The session passphrase is an Argon2id hash in .env, set by a first-run prompt, so even the passphrase itself is not stored in clear.'),
    ('h2', 'Backup, restore, and retention'),
    ('p', 'Backups are boring by design: a nightly job runs SQLite VACUUM INTO to a dated file in BACKUP_DIR and rsyncs the media vault (hard-linked where the filesystem allows, so dedup survives backup). The runbook (Ch. 10) contains the exact commands and the restore drill - restoring is copying the backup database over dev.db and the media directory back into place, then running the integrity check endpoint. Retention keeps everything by default: audio is the irreplaceable asset and personal scale will not fill a disk for years (1,000 calls at ~30 MB normalized is ~30 GB). The one scheduled cleanup is cloud-side by nature: nothing - AMBER retains no cloud state. Exports (FR-024) are the user-controlled offboarding path: one JSON bundle with every class B-D datum, redacted per rule scope ALL, and a manifest of media file hashes so the bundle plus the media dir fully reconstructs the system.'),
    ('table', {
        'caption': 'Table 8.2 - Data management policy summary',
        'headers': ['Datum', 'Where it lives', 'Backed up', 'Retention', 'Egress'],
        'ratios': [0.22, 0.26, 0.14, 0.18, 0.20],
        'rows': [
            ['Original + normalized audio', 'MEDIA_DIR, content-addressed', 'Nightly sync', 'Forever (default)', 'Never'],
            ['Transcripts + segments', 'SQLite', 'Nightly VACUUM INTO', 'Forever', 'Redacted, per policy'],
            ['Insights, scorecards, drafts', 'SQLite', 'Nightly', 'Forever', 'Redacted, per policy'],
            ['Deals, contacts, notes', 'SQLite', 'Nightly', 'Forever', 'Never (min context in prompts)'],
            ['Provider runs + spend', 'SQLite', 'Nightly', 'Forever (audit trail)', 'Never'],
            ['API keys', '.env only', 'Excluded by policy', 'Until rotated', 'Never'],
            ['Session records', 'In-memory + cookie', 'No', '12 hours', 'Never'],
        ],
    }),
    ('p', 'One legal note, kept proportionate: recording-consent law varies by jurisdiction and by the other party\'s location, and it is the operator\'s responsibility, not the software\'s. AMBER\'s design choices reflect good practice - post-call upload means the operator consciously selects what enters the system, and nothing is captured silently. If your calls span jurisdictions with all-party consent rules, the honest path is verbal notice at the top of the call; the follow-up template in Settings includes an optional one-line recording disclosure for exactly this reason.'),

