    ('p', 'The first-run wizard is four steps and exists to make the prerequisites visible rather than assumed: (1) session passphrase set and confirmed; (2) provider health check - Ollama detected, cloud keys optional and individually skippable; (3) model provisioning - a button per required model that runs the right pull and shows progress (the catalog in Ch. 11 lists exact tags: whisper is bundled with the app, qwen3:8b, gpt-oss:20b if you have headroom, nomic-embed-text); (4) the calibration pass - the wizard runs the eval harness on the golden set to establish your machine\'s baseline RTF and quality numbers, which become the reference point for every future change. Skipping step 4 is allowed but the Settings screen keeps a gentle nag until it runs once.'),
    ('h2', 'Upgrade procedure'),
    ('p', 'Upgrades follow the same shape as installs because the data layer is designed for it: git pull; pnpm install --frozen-lockfile; prisma migrate deploy (additive-first per Ch. 4 policy); ollama pulls re-checked against the catalog (new model tags appear as suggestions, never auto-pull); pnpm build; restart. Before the migrate step, the updater takes an automatic safety backup (VACUUM INTO with a pre-upgrade tag) - a failed migration restores from it and stops, rather than half-upgrading. After any migration that bumps the schemaFeature flag, the eval harness runs automatically and reports; a red eval report after upgrade pins the cause to a schema or model change, not to your imagination.'),
    ('h2', 'Backup, restore, and monitoring'),
    ('p', 'Nightly backup is a scheduled job (cron or launchd, both templates shipped): VACUUM INTO BACKUP_DIR/amber-YYYYMMDD.db plus rsync --link-dest of MEDIA_DIR; the job keeps 30 daily snapshots and prunes older ones. Restore is the drill that must be rehearsed, not read: stop the app; move the current dev.db aside; copy the chosen snapshot to dev.db; rsync the matching media snapshot back; start the app; open /api/system/health and confirm db ok plus queueDepth; spot-check one call\'s transcript and audio. The runbook dry-run performs this end-to-end on a scratch directory. Monitoring is deliberately minimal: the health endpoint (db, disk, Ollama, queue depth) polled by a tiny menu-bar widget or a cron that writes to a log; provider health lives in the app itself (FR-021); and spend is on every footer (FR-022). There is no telemetry because there is no one to phone home to.'),
